{"archetypes":[{"name":"resume-state","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds a complex resumable state machine where precedence and hydration bugs can silently restart or skip work.","prompt_body":"Investigate the resume classification flow against the plan precedence rules, especially blocked continuation, checkout mismatch, fresh, open-pr, merged, and done routing. Check whether counters, branch identity, PR identity, and durable flags are restored before any state write or side-effectful operation on non-fresh paths. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"github-authority","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"Normal-repo routes depend on GitHub PR state and head-ref verification, which is easy to misclassify.","prompt_body":"Review the integration between resume routing and gh.pr_view results for OPEN, MERGED, CLOSED, wrong-head, and exception cases. Verify that normal repositories treat successful GitHub responses as authoritative while intentionally gh-skipped contexts use only the allowed local-state ordering. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"state-persistence","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"Multiple state writers now thread counters, phase, durable flags, and continuation markers through terminal and resume paths.","prompt_body":"Examine all ship state and finalize state writes for consistency with the intended lifecycle. Pay particular attention to preserving counters and resume markers, avoiding zero-counter rewrites, writing postmerge before postmerge work, and writing done only after postmerge success. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"cap-loop","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The CI loop cap ordering and post-monitor counter increments have subtle off-by-one and handback risks.","prompt_body":"Inspect the CI monitor loop for cap-order behavior, post-monitor counter persistence, and terminal handback behavior. Verify that pass or already-merged outcomes at the cap are observed before stalling, and that did-fixing and transient-rerun increments are persisted consistently. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"statefile-hardening","focus_area":"security","weight":1,"rationale":"The Python driver reads and rewrites shell-style state files containing repo, branch, path, and conflict metadata.","prompt_body":"Review state-file parsing and writing as an untrusted input boundary. Check tmpdir containment, newline handling, allowed extra fields, conflict-file validation, repo slug validation, and whether any persisted value could create shell injection or path traversal risk when later sourced or consumed. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
